window.__DOCS_INDEX__=[{"p":"Overview","g":"Get started","t":"Overview","u":"./","x":"What controller-sets does, which page you need, and how to install it."},{"p":"Overview","g":"Get started","t":"What it does for you","u":"./#why","x":"What it does for you# Every Express + Mongoose API writes the same controllers: list with filters, read one, create, update, delete — and each hand-written copy has its own bugs. controller-sets writes them once and lets you configure the rest. Less codeOne call gives a model six endpoints with filtering, search, sorting and pagination. No controller code to write or test.Secure by defaultNothing is filterable or sortable until you allow it. Mass assignment, NoSQL injection and regex attacks are closed off.Fast at any sizeCursor pagination serves page 1,000 of 200,000 records in under a millis"},{"p":"Overview","g":"Get started","t":"Which page do I need?","u":"./#where-to-go","x":""},{"p":"Overview","g":"Get started","t":"Less code, same result","u":"./#compare","x":""},{"p":"Overview","g":"Get started","t":"Installation","u":"./#installation","x":""},{"p":"Quickstart","g":"Get started","t":"Quickstart","u":"quickstart.html","x":"A working, protected products API in ten minutes."},{"p":"Quickstart","g":"Get started","t":"Before you start","u":"quickstart.html#before","x":""},{"p":"Quickstart","g":"Get started","t":"Build a products API","u":"quickstart.html#demo","x":""},{"p":"Quickstart","g":"Get started","t":"What you get back","u":"quickstart.html#demo-responses","x":""},{"p":"Quickstart","g":"Get started","t":"What just happened","u":"quickstart.html#recap","x":""},{"p":"Quickstart","g":"Get started","t":"Where next","u":"quickstart.html#demo-next","x":""},{"p":"Core concepts","g":"Get started","t":"Core concepts","u":"concepts.html","x":"The four ideas every other page assumes: routers, allowlists, the request lifecycle and the response envelope."},{"p":"Core concepts","g":"Get started","t":"The three pieces","u":"concepts.html#pieces","x":""},{"p":"Core concepts","g":"Get started","t":"Nothing is open until you open it","u":"concepts.html#implicit-deny","x":""},{"p":"Core concepts","g":"Get started","t":"Fields no configuration can unlock","u":"concepts.html#fields-no-configuration-can-unlock","x":"Fields no configuration can unlock# Whatever you allow, these are stripped from every request body. They are database bookkeeping or MongoDB syntax, never client data: _id __v createdAt updatedAt any $-prefixed key any key containing . __proto__ constructor prototype To MongoDB, $ starts an operator and . reaches into a subdocument, so {\"$where\": \"…\"} or {\"owner.role\": \"admin\"} is an instruction, not data. __proto__ survives JSON.parse as a real key and can re-point an object's prototype. The check runs on nested values too, up to eight levels deep; anything deeper is refused."},{"p":"Core concepts","g":"Get started","t":"A request, step by step","u":"concepts.html#lifecycle","x":""},{"p":"Core concepts","g":"Get started","t":"One response envelope","u":"concepts.html#envelope","x":""},{"p":"Core concepts","g":"Get started","t":"How errors flow","u":"concepts.html#error-model","x":""},{"p":"Core concepts","g":"Get started","t":"Words used in these docs","u":"concepts.html#glossary","x":""},{"p":"createRouter","g":"Guides","t":"createRouter","u":"router.html","x":"Every option of createRouter — including file uploads — why you would use it, and every API endpoint it creates."},{"p":"createRouter","g":"Guides","t":"Options available in createRouter","u":"router.html#router-options","x":""},{"p":"createRouter","g":"Guides","t":"Model & response","u":"router.html#opts-core","x":""},{"p":"createRouter","g":"Guides","t":"Filtering, search & sorting","u":"router.html#opts-read","x":""},{"p":"createRouter","g":"Guides","t":"Pagination & limits","u":"router.html#opts-paging","x":""},{"p":"createRouter","g":"Guides","t":"Writing & validation","u":"router.html#opts-write","x":""},{"p":"createRouter","g":"Guides","t":"Cache","u":"router.html#opts-cache","x":""},{"p":"createRouter","g":"Guides","t":"Router options","u":"router.html#opts-router","x":""},{"p":"createRouter","g":"Guides","t":"S3 uploads","u":"router.html#s3-router","x":""},{"p":"createRouter","g":"Guides","t":"Sending files","u":"router.html#sending-files","x":"Sending files# curlBrowser (fetch) curl -X POST localhost:3000/api/products \\ -F name=Chair -F price=120 \\ -F cover=@cover.jpg \\ -F gallery=@side.jpg -F gallery=@back.jpg const form = new FormData(); form.append('name', 'Chair'); form.append('price', '120'); form.append('cover', coverInput.files[0]); for (const file of galleryInput.files) form.append('gallery', file); const res = await fetch('/api/products', { method: 'POST', body: form }); // no Content-Type header const { data } = await res.json(); console.log(data.cover, data.gallery); { \"success\": true, \"data\": { \"_id\": \"65af…\", \"name\": \"C"},{"p":"createRouter","g":"Guides","t":"API endpoints","u":"router.html#api-overview","x":""},{"p":"createRouter","g":"Guides","t":"Filtering & search in depth","u":"router.html#reading","x":""},{"p":"createRouter","g":"Guides","t":"Why filterable fields are allowlisted","u":"router.html#why-filterable-fields-are-allowlisted","x":"Why filterable fields are allowlisted# Suppose your schema has passwordResetToken and your API never returns it. If a client could send ?compareField=passwordResetToken&compareOperator=gt&compareValue=m, the number of results answers \"does the token sort after m?\" — and a few hundred such requests rebuild the token character by character. The value never appears in a response; it leaks through the count. Allowlisting closes that."},{"p":"createRouter","g":"Guides","t":"Searching a referenced model","u":"router.html#searching-a-referenced-model","x":"Searching a referenced model# A search field with a dot is split at the first dot. The part before must be a field declared with ref; the rest is the path on the referenced model. The library finds matching documents there, then filters your collection by their ids — no aggregation pipeline. Search fieldResult category.nameSearches name on the Category model author.profile.nameSearches profile.name on the Author model notARef.nameClause dropped — the field has no ref If a relational clause matches nothing, the result is empty, not \"search ignored\" — otherwise a search for something nobody sell"},{"p":"createRouter","g":"Guides","t":"Search terms are text, not patterns","u":"router.html#search-terms-are-text-not-patterns","x":"Search terms are text, not patterns# Regex metacharacters are escaped, so ?s=c++ finds the literal string. An unescaped term is a program the client writes and your database runs: ?s=(a+)+$ triggers catastrophic backtracking inside mongod. Terms over maxSearchLength (128) are rejected with 400. If trusted internal tools truly need patterns, allowRawRegex: true exists — behind authentication only."},{"p":"createRouter","g":"Guides","t":"How writes are filtered","u":"router.html#writing","x":""},{"p":"createRouter","g":"Guides","t":"What a client sends vs. what gets saved","u":"router.html#what-a-client-sends-vs-what-gets-saved","x":"What a client sends vs. what gets saved# // allowedFields: ['name', 'email'] // The client POSTs: { \"name\": \"Ayesha\", \"email\": \"ayesha@example.com\", \"role\": \"admin\", // ✘ not allowlisted → dropped \"_id\": \"65af…\", // ✘ always immutable → dropped \"$where\": \"1 === 1\" // ✘ MongoDB syntax → dropped } // What reaches the database: { \"name\": \"Ayesha\", \"email\": \"ayesha@example.com\" } Dropped fields are dropped quietly and the request succeeds with what's left. If nothing is left, the response is 400 \"Request body contains no writable fields.\" rather than an empty record. Mongoose strict mode is not a "},{"p":"createRouter","g":"Guides","t":"Everything about validate","u":"router.html#validation","x":""},{"p":"createRouter","g":"Guides","t":"What the hook receives","u":"router.html#what-the-hook-receives","x":"What the hook receives# ArgumentHolds payloadThe body, already filtered to writable fields. Never empty — an empty one is a 400 before the hook runs. context.req · context.resThe Express request and response — req.auth, headers, uploaded files. context.operation'create' or 'update'. context.idThe target document's id on update; undefined on create. context.modelThe Mongoose model."},{"p":"createRouter","g":"Guides","t":"What it may return or throw","u":"router.html#what-it-may-return-or-throw","x":"What it may return or throw# The hook…Result returns nothingThe filtered payload is saved as-is. returns an objectThat object is saved instead. $-prefixed, dotted and __proto__ keys are still stripped from it. throws ValidationError(message, fields?, status?)400 (or status) with a fields map. throws HttpError(status, message)That status and message — e.g. 403. throws anything else500; the detail is logged, not sent. is asyncAwaited — database checks are fine. The client sees: // 400 Bad Request { \"success\": false, \"error\": \"Check the submitted values.\", \"fields\": { \"price\": \"must be zero or mo"},{"p":"createRouter","g":"Guides","t":"Everything about runAfterCreate","u":"router.html#after-create","x":""},{"p":"createRouter","g":"Guides","t":"Everything about onGet","u":"router.html#on-get","x":""},{"p":"createRouter","g":"Guides","t":"Pagination in depth","u":"router.html#large-collections","x":""},{"p":"createRouter","g":"Guides","t":"Why offsets slow down","u":"router.html#why-offsets-slow-down","x":"Why offsets slow down# ?page=4000 asks MongoDB to walk and discard the 199,950 index entries before it — and the countDocuments that produces totals scans every match. Measured on 200,000 documents, 50 per page: page 1page 1,000page 4,000 'offset'18.7 ms19.1 ms47.8 ms 'cursor'3.6 ms0.6 ms0.9 ms Most of the offset cost is the count, which is why page 1 costs as much as page 1,000. Need page numbers but not totals? Keep offset mode and set countStrategy: 'none' — responses carry hasMore instead of totalPages."},{"p":"createRouter","g":"Guides","t":"How cursors behave","u":"router.html#how-cursors-behave","x":"How cursors behave# Ties are handled. _id is appended to your sort automatically, so records sharing a sort value are never skipped or repeated at page boundaries. A cursor carries an id, not values. The server re-reads the anchor record, so a hand-crafted cursor can't range-filter a field you never made filterable. A cursor is tied to its sort. Reusing one with a different sort, or after its anchor record was deleted, is a 400 — restart the scan. Modes don't mix. ?page= in cursor mode or ?cursor= in offset mode is a 400. None of this replaces an index. A cursor page is one index seek only if "},{"p":"createRouter","g":"Guides","t":"Add your own endpoints alongside","u":"router.html#extra-endpoints","x":""},{"p":"Protecting routes","g":"Guides","t":"Protecting routes","u":"protect.html","x":"Guards, public/admin splits, server-owned fields and per-user data."},{"p":"Protecting routes","g":"Guides","t":"Add a guard","u":"protect.html#middlewares","x":""},{"p":"Protecting routes","g":"Guides","t":"Public reads, staff writes","u":"protect.html#public-read-admin-write","x":""},{"p":"Protecting routes","g":"Guides","t":"Server-owned fields","u":"protect.html#owner-from-token","x":""},{"p":"Protecting routes","g":"Guides","t":"Users see only their own records","u":"protect.html#only-my-records","x":""},{"p":"Protecting routes","g":"Guides","t":"Rate limits","u":"protect.html#rate-limits","x":""},{"p":"Protecting routes","g":"Guides","t":"Before you go to production","u":"protect.html#checklist","x":""},{"p":"File uploads","g":"Guides","t":"File uploads","u":"uploads.html","x":"Accept files on a router or your own route, store them in S3, and compress images."},{"p":"File uploads","g":"Guides","t":"Setup","u":"uploads.html#upload-setup","x":""},{"p":"File uploads","g":"Guides","t":"Files on a CRUD router","u":"uploads.html#upload-router","x":""},{"p":"File uploads","g":"Guides","t":"Files on your own routes","u":"uploads.html#upload-middleware","x":""},{"p":"File uploads","g":"Guides","t":"What happens to a file","u":"uploads.html#uploads","x":""},{"p":"File uploads","g":"Guides","t":"Image compression","u":"uploads.html#images","x":""},{"p":"File uploads","g":"Guides","t":"When it fails","u":"uploads.html#upload-errors","x":""},{"p":"Caching","g":"Guides","t":"Caching","u":"cache.html","x":"Answer repeated reads from Redis, cleared automatically on every write."},{"p":"Caching","g":"Guides","t":"Turn it on","u":"cache.html#cache-setup","x":""},{"p":"Caching","g":"Guides","t":"What gets cached","u":"cache.html#cache-how","x":""},{"p":"Caching","g":"Guides","t":"Options","u":"cache.html#cache-options","x":""},{"p":"Caching","g":"Guides","t":"One connection for many routers","u":"cache.html#cache-shared-store","x":""},{"p":"Caching","g":"Guides","t":"Per-user data","u":"cache.html#cache-vary","x":""},{"p":"Caching","g":"Guides","t":"Changes made elsewhere","u":"cache.html#cache-invalidate","x":""},{"p":"Caching","g":"Guides","t":"When Redis is down","u":"cache.html#cache-down","x":""},{"p":"Caching","g":"Guides","t":"Without Redis (development)","u":"cache.html#cache-local","x":""},{"p":"Caching","g":"Guides","t":"Environment","u":"cache.html#cache-env","x":""},{"p":"Authentication","g":"Guides","t":"Authentication","u":"auth.html","x":"Sign-up, login, roles, refresh tokens and social sign-in on your own user model."},{"p":"Authentication","g":"Guides","t":"Setup","u":"auth.html#auth-setup","x":""},{"p":"Authentication","g":"Guides","t":"What your model needs","u":"auth.html#auth-model","x":""},{"p":"Authentication","g":"Guides","t":"Endpoints","u":"auth.html#auth-endpoints","x":""},{"p":"Authentication","g":"Guides","t":"Refresh tokens","u":"auth.html#auth-refresh","x":""},{"p":"Authentication","g":"Guides","t":"Rotation on or off","u":"auth.html#rotation-on-or-off","x":"Rotation on or off# rotate: true · defaultA new token on every refreshThe old one stops working. If it's presented again, someone is replaying it — that session ends, and by default every other session of that user. Choose this for browsers and anywhere a token might leak. rotate: falseSame token until it expiresSimpler for clients that can't reliably store a new token each time. Still revocable by logout and password change. Choose this for trusted server-to-server clients. Two tabs refreshing at once isn't an attack: a token rotated away in the last graceSeconds (10) is still accepted."},{"p":"Authentication","g":"Guides","t":"Client code","u":"auth.html#client-code","x":"Client code# Keep both tokens; on a 401, refresh once and retry. Save the refresh token you get back every time — it changes when rotating — and share one in-flight refresh between concurrent requests. let access = null; let refreshing = null; async function refresh() { refreshing ??= fetch('/api/auth/token/refresh', { method: 'POST', headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' }, body: JSON.stringify({ refreshToken: localStorage.getItem('refreshToken') }), }) .then(async (res) => { if (!res.ok) throw new Error('signed out'); const { data } = await res.json(); access = data.token; localStora"},{"p":"Authentication","g":"Guides","t":"How sessions are protected","u":"auth.html#how-sessions-are-protected","x":"How sessions are protected# Refresh tokens are opaque random values, not JWTs. Only an HMAC of each is stored, so a leaked database holds no usable token. Password change and reset revoke every refresh token. Sessions are capped at maxSessions (5) per user; the oldest is dropped. Every failure is the same 401 — unknown, expired, tampered and reused are indistinguishable. A refresh re-reads the user, so a role change or disabled account takes effect at the next refresh. OptionEnvDefault refresh.enabledAUTH_REFRESH_ENABLEDfalse refresh.rotateAUTH_REFRESH_ROTATEtrue refresh.expiresInAUTH_REFRESH_"},{"p":"Authentication","g":"Guides","t":"Social sign-in","u":"auth.html#social","x":""},{"p":"Authentication","g":"Guides","t":"Sign in with email or phone","u":"auth.html#identifiers","x":""},{"p":"Authentication","g":"Guides","t":"Rename, remove and guard routes","u":"auth.html#auth-urls","x":""},{"p":"Authentication","g":"Guides","t":"Rate-limit sign-in","u":"auth.html#rate-limit","x":""},{"p":"Authentication","g":"Guides","t":"What it refuses","u":"auth.html#auth-refuses","x":""},{"p":"Authentication","g":"Guides","t":"Complete example","u":"auth.html#full-auth","x":""},{"p":"Authentication","g":"Guides","t":"All options","u":"auth.html#auth-options-all","x":""},{"p":"Email & SMS","g":"Guides","t":"Email & SMS","u":"email.html","x":"Deliver password-reset codes with your own transporter, sender and templates."},{"p":"Email & SMS","g":"Guides","t":"Setup","u":"email.html#email-basics","x":""},{"p":"Email & SMS","g":"Guides","t":"How a reset works","u":"email.html#password-reset","x":""},{"p":"Email & SMS","g":"Guides","t":"Common options","u":"email.html#email-options","x":""},{"p":"Email & SMS","g":"Guides","t":"Swap the sender","u":"email.html#senders","x":""},{"p":"Email & SMS","g":"Guides","t":"Custom templates","u":"email.html#templates","x":""},{"p":"Email & SMS","g":"Guides","t":"SMTP transporter","u":"email.html#mail","x":""},{"p":"Email & SMS","g":"Guides","t":"Choosing email or SMS","u":"email.html#channels","x":""},{"p":"Custom routes","g":"Guides","t":"Custom routes","u":"custom-routes.html","x":"Use the ControllerSets handlers on routes you wire yourself."},{"p":"Custom routes","g":"Guides","t":"When to wire routes yourself","u":"custom-routes.html#adv-intro","x":""},{"p":"Custom routes","g":"Guides","t":"The six handlers","u":"custom-routes.html#adv-methods","x":""},{"p":"Custom routes","g":"Guides","t":"A router by hand","u":"custom-routes.html#adv-router","x":""},{"p":"Custom routes","g":"Guides","t":"Add logic before a handler","u":"custom-routes.html#adv-wrap","x":""},{"p":"Custom routes","g":"Guides","t":"Other members","u":"custom-routes.html#adv-helpers","x":""},{"p":"Custom routes","g":"Guides","t":"How the handlers are built","u":"custom-routes.html#internals","x":""},{"p":"HTTP API","g":"Reference","t":"HTTP API","u":"http-api.html","x":"For client developers: every endpoint, query parameter, QUERY body, response shape and status code."},{"p":"HTTP API","g":"Reference","t":"Endpoints","u":"http-api.html#endpoints","x":""},{"p":"HTTP API","g":"Reference","t":"Filtering","u":"http-api.html#api-filtering","x":""},{"p":"HTTP API","g":"Reference","t":"Search","u":"http-api.html#api-search","x":""},{"p":"HTTP API","g":"Reference","t":"Sorting","u":"http-api.html#api-sorting","x":""},{"p":"HTTP API","g":"Reference","t":"Pagination","u":"http-api.html#api-pagination","x":""},{"p":"HTTP API","g":"Reference","t":"Offset: page numbers","u":"http-api.html#offset-page-numbers","x":"Offset: page numbers# GET /api/products?page=2&pageSize=20 { \"success\": true, \"data\": [ … 20 records … ], \"pagination\": { \"currentPage\": 2, \"pageSize\": 20, \"totalPages\": 7, \"totalRecords\": 132 } } You sendYou get no pageAn unpaginated list, no pagination block, capped at the server's maxLimit (100 by default). page=150 records (the default page size). page=1&pageSize=5000Clamped to maxLimit. page=abc or page=-3Treated as page 1. If the server skips counting, pagination carries hasMore instead of totalPages and totalRecords."},{"p":"HTTP API","g":"Reference","t":"Cursor: follow nextCursor","u":"http-api.html#cursor-follow-nextcursor","x":"Cursor: follow nextCursor# When the server uses pagination: 'cursor', there are no page numbers. Request the first page with no cursor, then pass back nextCursor until hasMore is false: GET /api/events?pageSize=50 → { \"data\": [ … ], \"pagination\": { \"pageSize\": 50, \"hasMore\": true, \"nextCursor\": \"eyJpIjoi…\" } } GET /api/events?pageSize=50&cursor=eyJpIjoi… → … until \"hasMore\": false and \"nextCursor\": null Keep the same sort and filters for the whole scan; changing sort mid-scan is a 400. If the record a cursor points at was deleted, you get a 400 — restart from the first page. Sending page to a "},{"p":"HTTP API","g":"Reference","t":"Putting it together","u":"http-api.html#api-combined","x":""},{"p":"HTTP API","g":"Reference","t":"QUERY: reads with a JSON body","u":"http-api.html#api-query","x":""},{"p":"HTTP API","g":"Reference","t":"Body keys","u":"http-api.html#body-keys","x":"Body keys# KeyTypeMeaning filterobjectField → condition. Every field must be in filterableFields — being in query alone isn't enough if filterableFields is set. searchstringSame as ?s=. sortstring · string[]\"-price\", or up to five keys, each in sortableFields. pageinteger ≥ 1Paginated response. Not with limit. pageSizeinteger ≥ 1Per page, clamped to maxLimit. Alone, implies page: 1. limitinteger ≥ 1Cap an unpaginated read, clamped to maxLimit. cursorstringNext page, on cursor-mode servers. Every key is optional; an empty body is a plain list. Unknown keys are a 400, so a \"filters\" typo never s"},{"p":"HTTP API","g":"Reference","t":"Operators","u":"http-api.html#operators","x":"Operators# OperatorTakesMatches eq · nea valueEqual · not equal gt · gtea valueGreater than · or equal lt · ltea valueLess than · or equal in · nin1–100 valuesAny of · none of Operators are spelled without $. Values are strings, numbers, booleans or null, cast by the schema — \"gte\": \"50\" on a Number field works. Nested objects, empty conditions and unknown operators are a 400."},{"p":"HTTP API","g":"Reference","t":"The same read, both ways","u":"http-api.html#the-same-read-both-ways","x":"The same read, both ways# Query stringQUERY body ?category=65af…{ \"filter\": { \"category\": \"65af…\" } } ?tag=sale&tag=new{ \"filter\": { \"tag\": [\"sale\", \"new\"] } } ?rangeField=price&range=10-100{ \"filter\": { \"price\": { \"gte\": 10, \"lte\": 100 } } } ?compareField=price&compareOperator=gt&compareValue=50{ \"filter\": { \"price\": { \"gt\": 50 } } } ?s=chair{ \"search\": \"chair\" } ?sort=-price{ \"sort\": \"-price\" } — not expressible{ \"sort\": [\"-price\", \"name\"] } ?page=2&pageSize=20{ \"page\": 2, \"pageSize\": 20 }"},{"p":"HTTP API","g":"Reference","t":"Sending it","u":"http-api.html#sending-it","x":"Sending it# fetchcurlaxios Browsers and Node's fetch send QUERY as written — see the example above. curl -X QUERY http://localhost:3000/api/products \\ -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\ -d '{\"filter\":{\"price\":{\"gte\":50}},\"sort\":\"-price\",\"pageSize\":20}' const { data } = await axios.request({ url: '/api/products', method: 'QUERY', headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' }, // set it: some versions omit it for unknown methods data: { filter: { tag: ['sale', 'new'] }, sort: ['-price', 'name'] }, });"},{"p":"HTTP API","g":"Reference","t":"Is it available?","u":"http-api.html#is-it-available","x":"Is it available?# OPTIONS on the collection answers: OPTIONS /api/products HTTP/1.1 HTTP/1.1 200 OK Allow: GET, HEAD, POST, QUERY Accept-Query: application/json No QUERY in Allow means the server turned it off, runs Node older than 22.2, or a proxy stripped it. Many proxies, CDNs and API gateways drop methods they don't recognise, so test through your real edge and keep GET as the fallback. Don't put a URL-keyed shared cache in front of QUERY — every request to a collection has the same URL."},{"p":"HTTP API","g":"Reference","t":"What's refused","u":"http-api.html#what-s-refused","x":"What's refused# RequestStatusWhy { \"filter\": { \"secretToken\": \"x\" } }400Not in filterableFields. { \"filter\": { \"$where\": \"…\" } }400$-prefixed or dotted keys are never field names. { \"filter\": { \"price\": { \"$gte\": 1 } } }400Spell it gte. { \"sort\": \"createdBy\" }400Not in sortableFields. { \"filters\": { … } }400Unknown key. { \"page\": \"2\" }400Must be a number. Body sent as text/plain, or no JSON parser on the server415The body must be parsed JSON. QUERY /api/products/65af…404Collection only. Use GET /:id."},{"p":"HTTP API","g":"Reference","t":"Read, create, update, delete","u":"http-api.html#api-single","x":""},{"p":"HTTP API","g":"Reference","t":"Sending files","u":"http-api.html#sending-files","x":"Sending files# On routers with uploads, send multipart/form-data: ordinary fields as text, files under the field names the server configured. Don't set Content-Type yourself — the browser adds the boundary. Browser (fetch)curl const form = new FormData(); form.append('name', 'Chair'); form.append('price', '120'); form.append('cover', coverInput.files[0]); for (const file of galleryInput.files) form.append('gallery', file); const res = await fetch('/api/products', { method: 'POST', body: form }); const { data } = await res.json(); data.cover; // \"https://…/products/1726…-51234.jpg\" data.gallery"},{"p":"HTTP API","g":"Reference","t":"Responses","u":"http-api.html#responses","x":""},{"p":"HTTP API","g":"Reference","t":"Status codes","u":"http-api.html#errors","x":""},{"p":"JavaScript API","g":"Reference","t":"JavaScript API","u":"api.html","x":"Every export of the package, with signatures and TypeScript types."},{"p":"JavaScript API","g":"Reference","t":"Every export","u":"api.html#exports","x":""},{"p":"JavaScript API","g":"Reference","t":"createRouter","u":"api.html#x-createrouter","x":""},{"p":"JavaScript API","g":"Reference","t":"ControllerSets","u":"api.html#x-controllersets","x":""},{"p":"JavaScript API","g":"Reference","t":"isQueryMethodSupported","u":"api.html#x-isquery","x":""},{"p":"JavaScript API","g":"Reference","t":"errorHandler","u":"api.html#x-errorhandler","x":""},{"p":"JavaScript API","g":"Reference","t":"HttpError and ValidationError","u":"api.html#x-httperror","x":""},{"p":"JavaScript API","g":"Reference","t":"fileUploadMiddleware and compressImage","u":"api.html#x-upload","x":""},{"p":"JavaScript API","g":"Reference","t":"Cache stores","u":"api.html#x-cache","x":""},{"p":"JavaScript API","g":"Reference","t":"createAuthRouter and identifier","u":"api.html#x-auth","x":""},{"p":"JavaScript API","g":"Reference","t":"Auth building blocks","u":"api.html#x-auth-low","x":""},{"p":"JavaScript API","g":"Reference","t":"TypeScript","u":"api.html#typescript","x":""},{"p":"Environment variables","g":"Reference","t":"Environment variables","u":"env.html","x":"Every variable the library reads, with ready-made setups for common providers."},{"p":"Environment variables","g":"Reference","t":"Loading variables","u":"env.html#env-how","x":""},{"p":"Environment variables","g":"Reference","t":"Every variable","u":"env.html#env-reference","x":""},{"p":"Environment variables","g":"Reference","t":"A complete .env.example","u":"env.html#env-full","x":""},{"p":"Environment variables","g":"Reference","t":"Setup: CRUD only","u":"env.html#env-crud","x":""},{"p":"Environment variables","g":"Reference","t":"Setup: auth","u":"env.html#env-auth","x":""},{"p":"Environment variables","g":"Reference","t":"Setup: email","u":"env.html#env-email","x":""},{"p":"Environment variables","g":"Reference","t":"Setup: Redis","u":"env.html#env-redis","x":""},{"p":"Environment variables","g":"Reference","t":"Setup: S3","u":"env.html#env-s3","x":""},{"p":"Troubleshooting","g":"Help","t":"Troubleshooting","u":"troubleshooting.html","x":"Symptoms, causes and fixes for the problems people hit first."},{"p":"Troubleshooting","g":"Help","t":"Check these first","u":"troubleshooting.html#ts-first","x":""},{"p":"Troubleshooting","g":"Help","t":"Writes","u":"troubleshooting.html#ts-writes","x":""},{"p":"Troubleshooting","g":"Help","t":"POST returns 400 \"Request body contains no writable fields.\"","u":"troubleshooting.html#post-returns-400-request-body-contains-no-writable-fields","x":"POST returns 400 \"Request body contains no writable fields.\"# Everything sent was filtered out. Causes, most likely first: the fields aren't in allowedFields; express.json() isn't mounted so the body was never parsed; the client sent only immutable fields like _id; on an upload router, the multipart field names don't match upload.fields."},{"p":"Troubleshooting","g":"Help","t":"A field silently doesn't save","u":"troubleshooting.html#a-field-silently-doesn-t-save","x":"A field silently doesn't save# It isn't in allowedFields (or it is in blockedFields, which wins), or it's misspelled there — TypeScript won't catch it. On a per-verb policy, check the right verb: { create: [...], update: [...] }. For files, the field must be writable too."},{"p":"Troubleshooting","g":"Help","t":"Startup warning: \"has no allowedFields/blockedFields configured\"","u":"troubleshooting.html#startup-warning-has-no-allowedfields-blockedfields-configure","x":"Startup warning: \"has no allowedFields/blockedFields configured\"# That model's routes will write any schema field a client sends, including role or isAdmin. Add allowedFields. It prints once per model per process."},{"p":"Troubleshooting","g":"Help","t":"My validate hook causes a 500","u":"troubleshooting.html#my-validate-hook-causes-a-500","x":"My validate hook causes a 500# Usually a dereference of a field the client didn't send — payload.name.trim() when name is absent. The hook runs before the schema, so required fields may be missing, and on update every field is optional. To reject deliberately, throw ValidationError or HttpError; anything else is a 500."},{"p":"Troubleshooting","g":"Help","t":"A duplicate value returns 409","u":"troubleshooting.html#a-duplicate-value-returns-409","x":"A duplicate value returns 409# Working as intended: a unique index rejected the write. The message and fields name the conflicting field, so the client can show \"that email is already registered\"."},{"p":"Troubleshooting","g":"Help","t":"Is PUT supported?","u":"troubleshooting.html#is-put-supported","x":"Is PUT supported?# Not on generated routers — updates are PATCH and change only the fields sent. If a client needs PUT, add router.put('/:id', controller.update) on a hand-wired router."},{"p":"Troubleshooting","g":"Help","t":"Reads","u":"troubleshooting.html#ts-reads","x":""},{"p":"Troubleshooting","g":"Help","t":"I only get 100 records","u":"troubleshooting.html#i-only-get-100-records","x":"I only get 100 records# maxLimit caps every response so one request can't load a whole collection into memory. Paginate with ?page=, or raise it deliberately: maxLimit: 500."},{"p":"Troubleshooting","g":"Help","t":"A filter parameter is ignored","u":"troubleshooting.html#a-filter-parameter-is-ignored","x":"A filter parameter is ignored# Equality parameters for fields not in query are ignored rather than rejected. Add the field to query."},{"p":"Troubleshooting","g":"Help","t":"?sort=name returns 400","u":"troubleshooting.html#sort-name-returns-400","x":"?sort=name returns 400# name isn't in sortableFields. When unset, it defaults to your query fields plus the orderBy field — which rarely includes name. List it explicitly."},{"p":"Troubleshooting","g":"Help","t":"A range or compare returns 400","u":"troubleshooting.html#a-range-or-compare-returns-400","x":"A range or compare returns 400# The field isn't in filterableFields. When unset it equals query; once you set it, it replaces that default, so include every field you want filterable."},{"p":"Troubleshooting","g":"Help","t":"Search returns nothing when the term clearly exists","u":"troubleshooting.html#search-returns-nothing-when-the-term-clearly-exists","x":"Search returns nothing when the term clearly exists# For a relational field like category.name, the part before the dot must be a schema field with ref, or the clause is dropped — and if every clause drops, the result is deliberately empty. Also, terms match literally: regex syntax in the term matches nothing."},{"p":"Troubleshooting","g":"Help","t":"A field I hide with onGet sometimes appears","u":"troubleshooting.html#a-field-i-hide-with-onget-sometimes-appears","x":"A field I hide with onGet sometimes appears# If onGet throws, the read falls back to no select. Use select: false in the schema or a toJSON transform for anything that must never be returned — and keep lean off if you rely on toJSON."},{"p":"Troubleshooting","g":"Help","t":"How do I filter on a value the client must not choose?","u":"troubleshooting.html#how-do-i-filter-on-a-value-the-client-must-not-choose","x":"How do I filter on a value the client must not choose?# Set it in middleware. On Express 5 you can't assign to req.query — it's re-parsed on every read — so shadow it with Object.defineProperty, and set enableQuery: false so QUERY can't route around it. Full pattern: Users see only their own records."},{"p":"Troubleshooting","g":"Help","t":"QUERY requests","u":"troubleshooting.html#ts-query","x":""},{"p":"Troubleshooting","g":"Help","t":"QUERY returns 404","u":"troubleshooting.html#query-returns-404","x":"QUERY returns 404# In order of likelihood: the server runs Node older than 22.2, so the route wasn't mounted (check isQueryMethodSupported() and the startup warning); the request went to /:id — QUERY is collection-only; or a proxy, CDN or gateway refused the method before it reached the app."},{"p":"Troubleshooting","g":"Help","t":"QUERY returns everything, or a 400 I didn't expect","u":"troubleshooting.html#query-returns-everything-or-a-400-i-didn-t-expect","x":"QUERY returns everything, or a 400 I didn't expect# Everything: no filter arrived — the only filter key is filter, and misspelled top-level keys are 400s, so a 200 means the body was empty or unparsed. A 400: the message names the field or operator; usually a field missing from filterableFields / sortableFields, $gte instead of gte, or \"page\": \"2\" as a string."},{"p":"Troubleshooting","g":"Help","t":"QUERY returns 415","u":"troubleshooting.html#query-returns-415","x":"QUERY returns 415# The body wasn't JSON (Content-Type isn't application/json), or no JSON parser is mounted on the server."},{"p":"Troubleshooting","g":"Help","t":"When should I use QUERY instead of GET?","u":"troubleshooting.html#when-should-i-use-query-instead-of-get","x":"When should I use QUERY instead of GET?# When the parameters don't fit a URL: long id lists, several conditions per field, multi-key sorts, or filters you'd rather keep out of logs. For ordinary lists and shareable links, GET is cacheable and understood by everything. Both run identical code."},{"p":"Troubleshooting","g":"Help","t":"Uploads","u":"troubleshooting.html#ts-uploads","x":""},{"p":"Troubleshooting","g":"Help","t":"Upload routes return 503","u":"troubleshooting.html#upload-routes-return-503","x":"Upload routes return 503# S3 isn't configured. The server log names the missing variables — usually the environment wasn't loaded. The library never calls dotenv; add import 'dotenv/config' as your first import or run node --env-file=.env."},{"p":"Troubleshooting","g":"Help","t":"The uploaded file URL returns 403 in the browser","u":"troubleshooting.html#the-uploaded-file-url-returns-403-in-the-browser","x":"The uploaded file URL returns 403 in the browser# Uploads are private by default. Serve them through presigned URLs or a CDN with origin access, or set upload: { acl: 'public-read' } for content meant to be public."},{"p":"Troubleshooting","g":"Help","t":"An SVG (or other file type) is rejected","u":"troubleshooting.html#an-svg-or-other-file-type-is-rejected","x":"An SVG (or other file type) is rejected# Only JPEG, PNG, GIF, WebP, AVIF and PDF are allowed by default. Add the type to allowedMimeTypes. SVG can carry scripts, so it's always stored as attachment — converting icons to PNG or WebP is usually better."},{"p":"Troubleshooting","g":"Help","t":"Do I need sharp and the AWS SDK for CRUD only?","u":"troubleshooting.html#do-i-need-sharp-and-the-aws-sdk-for-crud-only","x":"Do I need sharp and the AWS SDK for CRUD only?# No. They load lazily, only when an upload is handled."},{"p":"Troubleshooting","g":"Help","t":"Authentication","u":"troubleshooting.html#ts-auth","x":""},{"p":"Troubleshooting","g":"Help","t":"Startup error about the token secret","u":"troubleshooting.html#startup-error-about-the-token-secret","x":"Startup error about the token secret# token.secret is missing or shorter than 32 characters. Generate one with node -e \"console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))\"."},{"p":"Troubleshooting","g":"Help","t":"Every request returns 401 after a password change","u":"troubleshooting.html#every-request-returns-401-after-a-password-change","x":"Every request returns 401 after a password change# Intended: tokens issued before a password change are rejected. Sign in again."},{"p":"Troubleshooting","g":"Help","t":"/password/forgot succeeds but no email arrives","u":"troubleshooting.html#password-forgot-succeeds-but-no-email-arrives","x":"/password/forgot succeeds but no email arrives# The endpoint answers the same whether or not the account exists, so check the server log. No transporter or sender configured means codes are never generated (a warning is logged at startup); a failing sender returns 503."},{"p":"Troubleshooting","g":"Help","t":"Setup","u":"troubleshooting.html#ts-setup","x":""},{"p":"Troubleshooting","g":"Help","t":"Can I use this from CommonJS?","u":"troubleshooting.html#can-i-use-this-from-commonjs","x":"Can I use this from CommonJS?# The package is ESM-only. From CommonJS: const cs = await import('express-controller-sets')."},{"p":"Troubleshooting","g":"Help","t":"Can one router serve two models?","u":"troubleshooting.html#can-one-router-serve-two-models","x":"Can one router serve two models?# No — one router, one model. Two routers on one model is common and recommended: public reads, staff writes."},{"p":"Troubleshooting","g":"Help","t":"Is legacyMode safe to leave on?","u":"troubleshooting.html#is-legacymode-safe-to-leave-on","x":"Is legacyMode safe to leave on?# No. It turns off field gating, restores raw regex and removes the read cap. It exists to deploy an upgrade today and tighten afterwards, and is removed in 4.0. See Upgrading."},{"p":"Upgrading","g":"Help","t":"Upgrading","u":"upgrading.html","x":"Deprecations in 3.3, and moving from 2.x to 3.x."},{"p":"Upgrading","g":"Help","t":"Which section applies to you","u":"upgrading.html#versions","x":""},{"p":"Upgrading","g":"Help","t":"Uploads move into createRouter (3.3)","u":"upgrading.html#from-3-2","x":""},{"p":"Upgrading","g":"Help","t":"Before 4.0","u":"upgrading.html#before-4","x":""},{"p":"Upgrading","g":"Help","t":"From 2.x to 3.x","u":"upgrading.html#from-2","x":""},{"p":"Upgrading","g":"Help","t":"Deploy today, tighten after","u":"upgrading.html#deploy-today-tighten-after","x":"Deploy today, tighten after# createRouter({ model: Product, legacyMode: true }); legacyMode restores 2.x behaviour so you can upgrade immediately, then remove it router by router as you add the options below. It re-enables every vulnerability listed, so don't leave it on."},{"p":"Upgrading","g":"Help","t":"What changed, and what to do","u":"upgrading.html#what-changed-and-what-to-do","x":"What changed, and what to do# What changedWhat to do Request bodies are filteredAdd allowedFields or blockedFields to every router. ?compareField=, ?rangeField=, ?sort= are allowlistedList fields in filterableFields / sortableFields, or rely on the query default. Search terms are escaped and length-cappedNothing — unless clients sent real patterns; then allowRawRegex: true, behind auth. Unpaginated reads capped at 100Paginate, or raise maxLimit deliberately. Relational search resolves the full nested pathRe-check depth-2 paths — they queried the wrong field before. String-form search reads ?s="},{"p":"Use with an LLM","g":"Help","t":"Use with an LLM","u":"llm.html","x":"One prompt so ChatGPT, Claude or Copilot write correct code for this package."},{"p":"Use with an LLM","g":"Help","t":"Why a prompt","u":"llm.html#llm-why","x":""},{"p":"Use with an LLM","g":"Help","t":"Why a prompt","u":"llm.html#llm-why","x":""},{"p":"Use with an LLM","g":"Help","t":"The prompt","u":"llm.html#prompt","x":""},{"p":"Use with an LLM","g":"Help","t":"The prompt","u":"llm.html#prompt","x":""},{"p":"Use with an LLM","g":"Help","t":"Save it in your project","u":"llm.html#save-it","x":""},{"p":"Use with an LLM","g":"Help","t":"Prompts that work well","u":"llm.html#ask","x":"Prompts that work well#After the guide, ask in plain language. Say what the data is, who may read it and who may change it — the model turns that into allowlists and guards. Build an Express API for a blog with express-controller-sets: - Post { title, body, tags[], author (ref User), published } - Anyone can list and read published posts, search title and body, filter by tag, newest first - Only signed-in users can create posts; author is set from the token, never the body - Only admins can delete - Email + password auth with refresh tokens (rotation on), password reset by email via SMTP Give "},{"p":"Use with an LLM","g":"Help","t":"Check what it wrote","u":"llm.html#verify","x":""}];
